$DIR/main.p:15:4: cannot store mk(i) in p.f, which outlives the function literal's frame
    mk(i) returns a closure, which it builds, with the variables it uses, in a
    region of its caller's frame, the function literal's (at main.p:15); they
    live as long as that frame (section 11.5). The function literal's frame is
    reclaimed each time the literal returns, but p.f belongs to the function
    around it and lives longer.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value itself instead of a view of the function literal's frame, or
    allocate it so that it lives as long as p.f.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:17:5: cannot store &W{} in p, which outlives the function literal's frame
    A composite literal whose address is taken (at main.p:17) is made in the
    function literal's frame, as a local variable would be. The function
    literal's frame is reclaimed each time the literal returns, but p belongs to
    the function around it and lives longer.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value itself instead of a view of the function literal's frame, or
    allocate it so that it lives as long as p.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
